            "pages": {
              "end": 3,
              "start": 3
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Theodore B. Olson, respondents contend that Ohio\u2019s franchise tax system, including its investment tax credit, is unwise, ineffective, and unconstitutional."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Theodore B. Olson",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "4906558c4deb55aeede93c723ac2fb629c3e5ce686414d54f688ec7c95dcc40c",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-4906558c4deb-0000-0000"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 3,
              "start": 3
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Theodore B. Olson, respondents face two major obstacles: they cannot show a concrete, particularized injury from Ohio\u2019s investment tax credit, and their Dormant Commerce Clause challenge lacks merit."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Douglas R. Cole",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "4906558c4deb55aeede93c723ac2fb629c3e5ce686414d54f688ec7c95dcc40c",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-4906558c4deb-0000-0002"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 13,
              "start": 13
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Douglas R. Cole, the Court has repeatedly rejected generalized taxpayer grievances as a basis for standing."
      }
    ],
