              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 3,
                "start": 3
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to Theodore B. Olson, respondents argue that Ohio's franchise tax system, including the investment tax credit, is unconstitutional, and they contend that a facial Dormant Commerce Clause challenge is without merit."
        },
        {
          "attribution": "Douglas R. Cole",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "4906558c4deb55aeede93c723ac2fb629c3e5ce686414d54f688ec7c95dcc40c",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-4906558c4deb-0000-0002"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 13,
                "start": 13
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to Douglas R. Cole, respondents' grievance as taxpayers is a generalized public grievance, which the Court has repeatedly rejected as a basis for standing."
        },
        {
          "attribution": "Douglas R. Cole",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "4906558c4deb55aeede93c723ac2fb629c3e5ce686414d54f688ec7c95dcc40c",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-4906558c4deb-0000-0004"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 14,
                "start": 14
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to Douglas R. Cole, even if standing were found, there is disagreement about whether that standing extends to challenge the investment tax credit itself."
        }
